          "snippet": "starter: `// Validate tool name and read-only path before executing; MCP does not grant authority."
        },
        {
          "file": "src/data/track-agent-internals.ts",
          "line": 75,
          "snippet": "id: \"tool-schemas-mcp\","
        },
        {
          "file": "src/data/track-agent-internals.ts",
          "line": 76,
          "snippet": "title: \"Tool Schemas and MCP Servers\","
        },
        {
          "file": "src/data/track-agent-internals.ts",
          "line": 79,
          "snippet": "body: `A tool is a name, a description and a typed argument schema. Structured output uses JSON schema to require an object such as {name: 'read_file', path: 'README.md'} with required properties and no extras. Validate both the schema and the allowlisted path at execution time; well-formed JSON is not authorization. The model chooses among the schemas it is given, so a vague description causes wrong calls. Keep schemas narrow: a path, not a free-form shell string.\\n\\nThe Model Context Protocol (MCP) lets a server publish tools and resources over a standard interface. A client may expose a selected subset of the server's tools. Review that subset and permit only read-only resources in this lab; publishing a tool does not itself authorize execution.`,"
        },
        {
          "file": "src/data/track-agent-internals.ts",
          "line": 82,
          "snippet": "{ q: \"What should you do before connecting an MCP server?\", options: [\"Trust it because it is popular\", \"Review the published tools and expose only approved read-only capabilities\", \"Disable schema validation\"], answer: 1, explanation: \"The client and wrapper must restrict which published capabilities the model can actually use.\" },"
        }
      ]
    },
